 Spring Snow Crabapple delivers a spectacular display of pure white, lightly fragrant flowers in May. Uniquely, it is fruitless — eliminating litter and fireblight concerns associated with fruit. Disease-resistant dark green foliage is significantly lower maintenance than many other crabapples. Well adapted to the Front Range.
]

=== Watering

Use 2–3 × 2 gph emitters (~1.9–2.8 gal/cycle at the current 28 min/zone runtime). Distribute emitters around the drip line, not at the trunk — feeder roots are at the canopy edge. Second-season establishing tree with moderate-to-high water demand — consistent delivery through summer is important for tree health and next year's bloom.

=== Fertilization

Per soil profile, use N-K formula only. Use granular ammonium sulfate (21-0-0) as the preferred nitrogen source — it provides a mild acidification side benefit at your pH 7.27, improving micronutrient availability. Apply in early spring before leaf-out. Apply a chelated micronutrient blend in May — crabapple is susceptible to iron and zinc deficiency in alkaline clay soils, and your soil test confirms both are very low. Avoid high-nitrogen fertilizers after mid-July. Compost mulch around the drip line benefits soil health over time.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Prune in late winter (February) while dormant. Focus on removing dead, crossing, or rubbing branches and maintaining an open canopy for air circulation. Remove water sprouts (vertical shoots from major branches) and root suckers throughout the season as they appear. Keep the center open for air circulation — this is the primary disease-prevention pruning goal.

=== Wildlife Value

Spring Snow Crabapple is fruitless, so it provides no fall berry food value — but its abundant white spring flowers are an excellent nectar source for butterflies (especially swallowtails and whites), native bees, and hummingbirds passing through in May. The flowers coincide with early hummingbird arrival in Commerce City. House finches and other birds use the branching structure for shelter and nesting. Cottontail rabbits may chew the bark of young trunks in winter — protect with a hardware cloth cylinder. Field mice pose a similar bark-chewing risk; keep mulch away from the trunk base.

=== Pests & Disease

Although Spring Snow is #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=crabapple+fireblight+treatment")[fireblight]-resistant, monitor for symptoms (blackened 'shepherd's crook' shoot tips in spring) and prune infected wood 12 inches below visible damage with sterilized tools. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=apple+scab+crabapple+treatment")[Apple scab] resistance is built in to this variety, but monitor for dark spots on leaves in wet springs. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=crabapple+aphids+treatment")[Aphids] can cluster on new growth — treat with insecticidal soap or neem oil spray. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=tent+caterpillars+treatment")[Tent caterpillars] may appear in spring — remove egg masses and tent webs by hand; treat with Bt (Bacillus thuringiensis) spray if extensive.

=== Other Care

Mulch 2–3 inches deep. Wrap young trunks through the first few winters to prevent sunscald. Sterilize pruning tools with 10% bleach solution before and after any fireblight-related cuts to prevent spreading the bacteria. Clean up any dropped leaves or debris in fall to reduce overwintering disease inoculum.
